2
00:00:32,121 --> 00:00:33,561
Oh, heavens.

3
00:00:33,601 --> 00:00:36,161
This is nice. It's from Gwen.

4
00:00:36,281 --> 00:00:37,281
She's married.

5
00:00:37,321 --> 00:00:39,601
Who's Gwen? She used to work as a
housemaid here.

6
00:00:39,641 --> 00:00:40,921
She left to be a secretary.

7
00:00:42,401 --> 00:00:45,401
That's her ladyship for you, Miss
Braithwaite.

8
00:00:45,601 --> 00:00:48,601
She says they kept it very quiet
because his mother's ill

9
00:00:48,641 --> 00:00:51,881
but she hopes she can introduce us to
him in due course.

10
00:00:51,921 --> 00:00:53,161
Well, I think it's lovely.

11
00:00:53,201 --> 00:00:56,001
We should send her a card. I'll get
one and we can all sign it.

12
00:00:57,801 --> 00:01:00,321
Sorry. I nearly forgot this.
No harm done.

13
00:01:00,361 --> 00:01:04,521
How are you finding it? Have that
lot got used to your promotion yet?

14
00:01:04,561 --> 00:01:07,081
Some of them. But not all, eh?

15
00:01:07,121 --> 00:01:10,161
I'll be fine. I don't need to have
everyone love me.

16
00:01:10,521 --> 00:01:13,081
Nor me. Which is just as well...

17
00:01:16,881 --> 00:01:18,881
Miss Braithwaite?

18
00:01:19,041 --> 00:01:21,841
I hope you don't mind
but can I give you some advice?

19
00:01:22,281 --> 00:01:27,641
Go on. Mr Barrow never bothered with you
when you were a housemaid but he will now.

20
00:01:27,681 --> 00:01:30,601
I should be friendly but keep him at
arms length if I were you.

21
00:01:32,041 --> 00:01:34,041
I'd better get on.

22
00:01:40,361 --> 00:01:41,681
What do you think's in it?

23
00:01:41,721 --> 00:01:43,561
I cannot say, James.

24
00:01:43,601 --> 00:01:47,121
It's addressed to Lady Mary so
perhaps you could question HER later?

25
00:01:48,521 --> 00:01:50,041
Mr Carson?

26
00:01:53,401 --> 00:01:57,681
You see the box has been delivered
from the late Mr Crawley's office? Yes.

27
00:01:57,721 --> 00:02:02,281
Well, ought we to give it straight to Lady
Mary? Shouldn't someone else see it first?

28
00:02:02,321 --> 00:02:05,161
In case there's something in it
to make her cry.

29
00:02:05,201 --> 00:02:09,001
You may be right. I'll take it to
his lordship and he can decide.

30
00:02:22,441 --> 00:02:25,881
I say. Phyllis Dare's coming to the
Theatre Royal in York.

31
00:02:25,921 --> 00:02:30,201
Miss Dare will appear in The Lady Of The Rose
which was a hit musical of the London season.

32
00:02:30,241 --> 00:02:33,761
Who's Phyllis Dare?
Only one of the Dare sisters.

33
00:02:34,441 --> 00:02:37,121
Zena and Phyllis Dare Up here in
York.

34
00:02:37,161 --> 00:02:40,681
What happened to the other one. She
married the son of Lord Esher then retired.

35
00:02:40,721 --> 00:02:42,321
Oh, all right for some!

36
00:02:42,361 --> 00:02:46,121
Have you truly never heard of them?
Why would I? I don't go to the theatre.

37
00:02:46,161 --> 00:02:49,241
You must have been sometimes?
No, I've never been.

38
00:02:49,281 --> 00:02:51,481
She's got better things to spend her
money on.

39
00:02:56,281 --> 00:03:00,281
I don't understand you. Of course
you must give it to her.

40
00:03:00,321 --> 00:03:02,321
But what if it has no legal status?

41
00:03:02,361 --> 00:03:05,441
I don't want to build her hopes up
only to have them dashed.

42
00:03:05,481 --> 00:03:09,561
Robert, Matthew intended Mary to be
his sole heiress.

43
00:03:09,601 --> 00:03:14,401
That will mean a great deal to her
whether or not it's legal.

44
00:03:14,441 --> 00:03:17,601
Can't you see that?
But is it right to exclude George?

45
00:03:17,641 --> 00:03:20,561
Well, right or not,
it is what he wanted.

46
00:03:21,521 --> 00:03:23,521
Besides, it's not up to you.

47
00:03:28,721 --> 00:03:30,001
I'll send it to Murray.

48
00:03:30,041 --> 00:03:33,441
Well, Mary must read it before you
send it anywhere.

49
00:03:33,481 --> 00:03:36,721
Or are you trying to hide from the
truth?

50
00:03:36,761 --> 00:03:39,121
What truth?

51
00:03:39,161 --> 00:03:44,961
That you would prefer to be in sole charge of
the estate and not share the crown with Mary.

52
00:03:45,001 --> 00:03:47,921
Don't be silly. This won't make any
difference to all that.

53
00:03:47,961 --> 00:03:49,921
She won't want to get involved.

54
00:03:49,961 --> 00:03:56,401
When you talk like that, I'm tempted
to ring for Nanny and have you put
to bed with no supper.

55
00:04:00,201 --> 00:04:02,921
Goodbye, Mr Grigg.
Thank you, Doctor.

56
00:04:04,081 --> 00:04:07,201
All he needs to put him right
is some paid work.

57
00:04:07,241 --> 00:04:13,321
It's hard for a healthy young man to
find a job these days, never mind
poor old Mr Grigg.

58
00:04:13,361 --> 00:04:14,841
I know. Thank you.

59
00:04:14,881 --> 00:04:17,281
But Mrs Crawley has things in hand.

60
00:04:19,081 --> 00:04:22,041
My guess is that you knew what you
were doing bringing him here.

61
00:04:22,081 --> 00:04:26,161
Mrs Hughes? Won't you come up?

62
00:04:28,841 --> 00:04:31,041
Oh, don't get up for me.

63
00:04:31,081 --> 00:04:38,161
What news of Charlie? Mr Carson is still
very busy but he sends his best wishes.

64
00:04:39,321 --> 00:04:40,961
Does he?

65
00:04:41,321 --> 00:04:43,241
How did he phrase that exactly?

66
00:04:44,081 --> 00:04:48,601
Well, he... He said to tell you -
He didn't say nothing, did he?

67
00:04:51,121 --> 00:04:58,001
You have to understand, he doesn't
remember the days you spent
together with any great nostalgia.

68
00:04:58,921 --> 00:05:03,081
He thinks it were all my fault...
but it weren't.

69
00:05:03,441 --> 00:05:04,881
Meaning what?

70
00:05:04,921 --> 00:05:05,921
Never mind.

71
00:05:07,681 --> 00:05:09,681
But it weren't my fault.

72
00:05:11,201 --> 00:05:14,561
They're all coming tonight so we
have to stretch a dinner by two places.

73
00:05:14,601 --> 00:05:17,081
Why, suddenly?
I don't know, do I?

74
00:05:17,121 --> 00:05:20,521
Go on about Jimmy. I just want Ivy
to know he's not interested in her.

75
00:05:21,401 --> 00:05:24,481
He pretends he is because he knows
it annoys me. How can you be so sure?

76
00:05:24,521 --> 00:05:27,121
He hasn't been bothered about her
before now, has he?

77
00:05:27,161 --> 00:05:28,721
Sometimes people come around.

78
00:05:28,761 --> 00:05:30,321
You hope.

79
00:05:30,361 --> 00:05:33,441
I thought the fish seller might be
in the village but he's not coming.

80
00:05:33,481 --> 00:05:36,761
I'll have to go to Tuttles in York.
I can do it.

81
00:05:37,281 --> 00:05:41,081
What? Go to York. You can tell Mr
Carson I'm there on your business.

82
00:05:41,121 --> 00:05:43,681
We don't know he can spare you.
He will if YOU ask him.

83
00:05:56,521 --> 00:05:57,521
Hello, Mr Molesley.

84
00:06:00,961 --> 00:06:04,321
How are things? Well, as you can see,
not very good.

85
00:06:04,361 --> 00:06:07,001
I don't agree. It's skilled work.

86
00:06:07,041 --> 00:06:08,801
No, it isn't, not what I'm doing.

87
00:06:08,841 --> 00:06:11,721
I'm sure if you just wait something
better will turn up.

88
00:06:11,761 --> 00:06:14,601
I have waited
and nothing's turned up.

89
00:06:14,641 --> 00:06:18,761
I haven't earned a penny since Mr Crawley died
and now I owe money all over the village.

90
00:06:19,601 --> 00:06:23,241
Yes, but surely with your -
Don't you understand? I'm at my wits' end!

91
00:06:25,161 --> 00:06:31,121
I apologise. I should not have said
that. It was vulgar and
self-important. Please, forgive me.

92
00:06:35,241 --> 00:06:36,601
How much do you owe?

93
00:06:38,521 --> 00:06:41,761
What difference does it make? 15, 20
pounds.

94
00:06:41,801 --> 00:06:43,761
More than I've any likelihood of.

95
00:06:43,801 --> 00:06:44,841
Mr Molesley...

96
00:06:46,641 --> 00:06:51,321
would it be useful if Mr Bates and I
lent you some money?

97
00:06:52,361 --> 00:06:56,161
When would I pay it back?
Give it, then.

98
00:06:56,201 --> 00:06:59,881
We'll give you some, if you like.
Not much but...but some.

99
00:07:00,361 --> 00:07:02,001
I couldn't accept that...

100
00:07:03,281 --> 00:07:06,881
..but I thank you, I do, most
sincerely.

101
00:07:06,921 --> 00:07:08,601
Get back to work!

102
00:07:16,081 --> 00:07:18,441
Anna said you wanted to see me
before I go down.

103
00:07:18,481 --> 00:07:22,161
Yes. That is...
Thank you, Bates, that will be all.

104
00:07:28,321 --> 00:07:32,881
You know I've invited Granny
and Isobel to dinner. I didn't but -

105
00:07:32,921 --> 00:07:36,081
Well, the thing is...

106
00:07:36,121 --> 00:07:39,441
There is a letter for you from
Matthew.

107
00:07:39,481 --> 00:07:43,201
What? Where was it?

108
00:07:43,241 --> 00:07:46,441
Hidden in a book in the office and
so it was overlooked.

109
00:07:46,481 --> 00:07:51,401
They finally got round to packing up his
things and brought them here this morning.

110
00:07:58,241 --> 00:08:01,041
Who opened it?
It wasn't sealed.

111
00:08:01,681 --> 00:08:04,361
But you've read it before me? Yes.

112
00:08:04,401 --> 00:08:06,721
I would have sent it to Murray to
have it looked into

113
00:08:06,761 --> 00:08:09,281
but your grandmother insisted you
should see it first.

114
00:08:09,321 --> 00:08:13,361
Granny's read it too? And what do
you mean, 'To have it looked into?'

115
00:08:13,401 --> 00:08:15,961
Read it. Then you'll understand.

116
00:08:25,441 --> 00:08:30,161
I felt so sorry for him. To be in
debt like that is the worst thing.

117
00:08:30,921 --> 00:08:33,761
Molesley's not having much luck.

118
00:08:33,801 --> 00:08:37,201
But if he won't accept help then...
I don't know.

119
00:08:39,041 --> 00:08:40,641
I'm really upset by it.

120
00:08:40,681 --> 00:08:42,681
Well, we can't have that.

121
00:08:45,201 --> 00:08:46,681
I don't understand.

122
00:08:46,721 --> 00:08:49,001
Did Matthew leave instructions after
all?

123
00:08:49,041 --> 00:08:52,321
He left a letter. He wrote it before
we went to Scotland.

124
00:08:53,321 --> 00:08:57,681
Well, where has it been? Concealed
in a book. They only dropped off today.

125
00:09:02,521 --> 00:09:04,521
Papa, you read it.

126
00:09:08,761 --> 00:09:11,481
My darling Mary, we are off to
Duneagle in the morning

127
00:09:11,521 --> 00:09:15,521
and I have suddenly realised that I've
never made a will or anything like one,

128
00:09:15,561 --> 00:09:20,521
which seems pretty feeble for a
lawyer and you being pregnant makes
it even more irresponsible.

129
00:09:20,561 --> 00:09:22,601
Hm, I'm afraid I have to agree with
that.

130
00:09:23,041 --> 00:09:27,761
I'll do it properly when I get back
and tear this up before you ever see it

131
00:09:27,801 --> 00:09:33,441
but I'll feel easier that I've
recorded on paper that I wish YOU to
be my sole heiress.

132
00:09:33,481 --> 00:09:35,681
What?

133
00:09:35,721 --> 00:09:39,881
I cannot know if our baby is a boy
or a girl but I do know it will be a baby,

134
00:09:39,921 --> 00:09:43,001
if anything happens to me before
I've drawn up a will

135
00:09:43,041 --> 00:09:44,801
and so you must take charge.

136
00:09:46,801 --> 00:09:51,281
And now I shall sign this and get
off home for dinner with you.

137
00:09:52,641 --> 00:09:56,241
What a lovely, lovely thought.
Matthew.

138
00:09:56,721 --> 00:10:01,041
Now you see
why I didn't want to read it.

139
00:10:01,521 --> 00:10:05,441
But surely it must be legal or do
there have to be witnesses?

140
00:10:05,481 --> 00:10:10,361
But it was witnessed by two of his
clients, which is why no-one in the
office knew it existed.

141
00:10:10,401 --> 00:10:13,041
Then it's settled. Nothing is
settled.

142
00:10:14,281 --> 00:10:18,361
This is why I wanted Murray to check
it first.

143
00:10:19,521 --> 00:10:23,281
Whatever Matthew's intentions, it is
not a will.

144
00:10:26,001 --> 00:10:29,601
I've explained to Mr Carson about
the fish and he says you can go.

145
00:10:29,641 --> 00:10:31,121
Thanks.

146
00:10:33,641 --> 00:10:37,041
A pound to a penny he's got some
trick up his sleeve. You're so suspicious.

147
00:10:37,081 --> 00:10:39,641
There are two reasons why he's being
so nice to Ivy.

148
00:10:39,681 --> 00:10:43,401
The first is to make me angry and I
dread to think about the second.

149
00:10:43,441 --> 00:10:47,881
And I have my reason for getting
THAT to the dining room before midnight.

150
00:10:50,281 --> 00:10:55,081
I don't exactly know why but I feel
very happy that Matthew's been
allowed a last word.

151
00:10:55,121 --> 00:10:57,721
I agree, more than I can say.

152
00:10:57,761 --> 00:10:59,761
I knew he'd have a sensible plan.

153
00:11:00,561 --> 00:11:02,401
I'm not sure how sensible it is.

154
00:11:02,441 --> 00:11:07,681
If the letter is valid, the estate
will have to pay death duties twice
before it reaches little George.

155
00:11:08,601 --> 00:11:12,601
But in the meantime it will have all
the benefit of Mary's interest.

156
00:11:13,401 --> 00:11:15,321
I hope you intend to get stuck in.

157
00:11:15,361 --> 00:11:18,521
I want the right to an opinion. I
shall be content with that.

158
00:11:18,561 --> 00:11:22,441
You already have a right to an
opinion. Do I? Good.

159
00:11:22,481 --> 00:11:27,561
Most certainly you do. In fact,
there's a question of using empty
farmyards as new sources of revenue.

160
00:11:27,601 --> 00:11:29,721
I'd like to know what you feel about
that.

161
00:11:29,761 --> 00:11:31,521
Well, I'd have to think about it -

162
00:11:31,561 --> 00:11:35,401
Crop rotation, livestock versus cereals,
or indeed the whole matter of the tax.

163
00:11:35,441 --> 00:11:37,961
There are lots of things I would
like your opinion on.

164
00:11:39,681 --> 00:11:42,841
I assume you're trying to make some
sort of point?

165
00:11:42,881 --> 00:11:45,321
He's trying to show that a woman's
place is in the home.

166
00:11:45,361 --> 00:11:47,241
But she knows a lot about Matthew's
plans.

167
00:11:47,281 --> 00:11:49,321
That has value for me.

168
00:11:49,361 --> 00:11:51,361
Mrs Crawley, what do you think?

169
00:11:51,401 --> 00:11:55,241
I'm afraid I'm on Mary's side,
Robert, if sides there must be.

170
00:11:55,281 --> 00:12:01,001
There are no sides, not at all. I'm
pleased if you're pleased. I'm just
saying YOU have some work to do.

171
00:12:01,041 --> 00:12:03,201
That is IF the letter turns out to
be valid.

172
00:12:04,201 --> 00:12:06,201
Which you very much hope it is not.

173
00:12:11,122 --> 00:12:12,882
What's the matter with you?

174
00:12:13,114 --> 00:12:15,734
I'm a stupid fool, that's what.
Oh, lordy.

175
00:12:16,115 --> 00:12:18,235
How did you manage that?

176
00:12:19,205 --> 00:12:23,405
Daydreaming. I don't know what I
was about. She'll be livid.

177
00:12:25,045 --> 00:12:27,005
Not if you do as I tell you. Come
on.

178
00:12:28,885 --> 00:12:31,805
By the way did you get that card
For Gwen? Yes, why?

179
00:12:31,845 --> 00:12:34,885
I thought it'd be nice for people in
the village to sign it.

180
00:12:36,085 --> 00:12:39,445
The Bakewells, Mr Molesley, that
sort of thing.

181
00:12:40,085 --> 00:12:42,845
I'll collect them. You surprise me.

182
00:12:42,885 --> 00:12:44,245
Why?

183
00:12:44,645 --> 00:12:46,405
I never think of you as social.

184
00:12:46,445 --> 00:12:48,965
Why should I be social,
when I have you?

185
00:12:51,765 --> 00:12:53,965
You wrote to the Opera House in
Belfast?

186
00:12:55,605 --> 00:12:59,005
That was enterprising.
I wrote to a great many theatres.

187
00:12:59,405 --> 00:13:01,885
But the manager at the Opera House
wrote back.

188
00:13:01,925 --> 00:13:04,485
It seems they're in need of a
stage door keeper.

189
00:13:05,045 --> 00:13:08,845
They'd chosen one but he dropped
out. He asks Mr Grigg to consider it.

190
00:13:12,805 --> 00:13:18,205
I heard about Mr Matthew's letter.
I hope it wasn't too upsetting.

191
00:13:19,565 --> 00:13:21,525
As a matter of fact, it was a
relief.

192
00:13:23,765 --> 00:13:28,365
I felt so happy that he'd finally
been heard

193
00:13:28,405 --> 00:13:33,245
but judging by tonight's dinner, it
may prove a heavy mantle for Lady Mary.

194
00:13:40,725 --> 00:13:45,405
I shall keep it on my dressing table
to remind me that Matthew is on my side.

195
00:13:46,805 --> 00:13:48,645
They're all on your side.

196
00:13:49,685 --> 00:13:51,845
Papa gave me such a whacking at
dinner.

197
00:13:52,605 --> 00:13:55,405
Your father loves you very much.

198
00:13:57,885 --> 00:14:01,405
He's also very glad to have Downton
back under his control.

199
00:14:02,725 --> 00:14:06,885
He always speaks highly of Mr
Matthew and the changes he made.

200
00:14:06,925 --> 00:14:11,485
I'm sure he is very genuine but he
thinks he can manage alone now

201
00:14:11,525 --> 00:14:14,125
and the question is, can he?

202
00:14:23,325 --> 00:14:25,205
You wanted to borrow a belt, m'lady?

203
00:14:25,645 --> 00:14:28,125
Lady Mary wondered if any of these
might suit?

204
00:14:28,165 --> 00:14:29,845
Thank you.

205
00:14:29,885 --> 00:14:33,085
Actually, I'm glad that you're here
as there's something I want to ask.

206
00:14:36,965 --> 00:14:39,725
Look what I found in the village
today.

207
00:14:39,765 --> 00:14:43,325
Don't you think it would be the most
terrific fun?

208
00:14:43,365 --> 00:14:45,125
Have you asked her ladyship?

209
00:14:45,165 --> 00:14:50,005
She'd never let me go, at least not
without a chaperone but Lady Edith
will be in London

210
00:14:50,045 --> 00:14:52,485
and Lady Mary's not in the mood.

211
00:14:52,525 --> 00:14:57,045
But is this quite suitable? It looks
more like something for servants and
farmworkers to me.

212
00:14:57,085 --> 00:15:00,285
So? They'll dance the one-step,
won't they?

213
00:15:00,325 --> 00:15:03,805
I've practiced and practiced and I'm
dying to try it.

214
00:15:04,445 --> 00:15:06,205
Won't you accompany me?

215
00:15:07,125 --> 00:15:10,805
Then if we're found out, I can say
that you came with me so nothing was amiss.

216
00:15:11,445 --> 00:15:15,005
Of course I can't, m'lady, not
without asking Lady Mary.

217
00:15:15,765 --> 00:15:19,365
But that would spoil everything.
She'd be bound to tell Lady Grantham.

218
00:15:20,045 --> 00:15:22,845
Please? I know you love dancing

219
00:15:22,885 --> 00:15:27,405
and Mr Bates may have many qualities
but he's not a dancer.

220
00:15:27,725 --> 00:15:29,565
No, m'lady, he isn't a dancer...

221
00:15:30,685 --> 00:15:33,565
but I'm afraid its quite impossible.

222
00:15:36,925 --> 00:15:40,245
But how did it happen?
I don't like to say, m'lady.

223
00:15:40,285 --> 00:15:42,685
I am truly vexed. It was a
favourite.

224
00:15:42,725 --> 00:15:45,085
I know, m'lady, and I'm very sorry.

225
00:15:45,125 --> 00:15:48,845
But you won't tell me how you did it.
I can't.

226
00:15:49,565 --> 00:15:53,365
Very well, Braithwaite. I suppose
you'd better look me out another.

227
00:16:09,685 --> 00:16:14,765
What are you doing? I'm just sorting some old
papers. I haven't looked at these in years.

228
00:16:14,805 --> 00:16:17,845
Why are you looking now?
No particular reason.

229
00:16:18,965 --> 00:16:22,245
Ah. I knew I hadn't thrown it away.

230
00:16:22,645 --> 00:16:25,805
Who was she? Just a friend at one
time.

231
00:16:27,365 --> 00:16:29,365
What was she called?

232
00:16:29,885 --> 00:16:32,405
Alice. Alice Neal.

233
00:16:33,085 --> 00:16:39,565
And you were fond of her? I was but
people drift in and out of your
life, don't they?

234
00:16:41,565 --> 00:16:44,205
Truth to tell, I felt she'd treated
me badly.

235
00:16:45,325 --> 00:16:47,365
What does it matter anyway?

236
00:16:47,405 --> 00:16:50,965
We shout and scream and wail and cry
but in the end we must all die.

237
00:16:52,405 --> 00:16:54,405
Well, that's cheered me up!

238
00:16:55,645 --> 00:16:57,765
Thank you. Now I'll get on with my
work.

239
00:17:01,125 --> 00:17:04,245
Your ladyship, Mr Bates.

240
00:17:06,565 --> 00:17:08,485
Bates?

241
00:17:08,525 --> 00:17:11,525
I am very sorry to interrupt you
when you're busy, m'lady.

242
00:17:11,565 --> 00:17:13,845
It concerns Mr Molesley the younger.

243
00:17:13,885 --> 00:17:16,645
You make him sound like a Greek
philosopher.

244
00:17:16,685 --> 00:17:21,885
He has fallen on hard times and I
know that YOU have helped him in the past.

245
00:17:23,805 --> 00:17:26,405
Are you asking me to give him some
money?

246
00:17:26,445 --> 00:17:31,485
I am asking for money, yes. Although
he's too proud to accept it as a gift.

247
00:17:31,525 --> 00:17:33,205
Oh, how refreshing.

248
00:17:33,245 --> 00:17:35,085
Although I think we can find a way.

249
00:17:37,245 --> 00:17:39,245
Morning, Barrow.

250
00:17:39,485 --> 00:17:41,565
Good morning, m'lady.

251
00:17:42,165 --> 00:17:46,125
Is his lordship in the library?
He is but erm...

252
00:17:46,165 --> 00:17:47,405
What is it?

253
00:17:48,845 --> 00:17:52,605
Miss Braithwaite is very unhappy.
With good reason.

254
00:17:52,645 --> 00:17:55,165
Yes, but you see...

255
00:17:55,205 --> 00:18:01,285
she couldn't explain how it happened because,
well, she didn't want to point the finger.

256
00:18:01,325 --> 00:18:02,965
Point the finger at whom?

257
00:18:03,005 --> 00:18:05,485
Well, that's just it, m'lady,
I dare not either.

258
00:18:06,965 --> 00:18:09,205
I'm in enough trouble with Mr Bates
as it is.

259
00:18:09,245 --> 00:18:11,205
You're not saying Bates is to blame?

260
00:18:11,245 --> 00:18:13,005
Not Mr Bates himself, no.

261
00:18:13,045 --> 00:18:15,925
Anna, then, but why would she do
such a thing?

262
00:18:15,965 --> 00:18:19,965
As to that, m'lady, who knows?
Perhaps it was an accident.

263
00:18:20,005 --> 00:18:23,205
Though they do say there's no-one so
jealous as a lady's maid.

264
00:18:23,245 --> 00:18:25,245
Cora, is that you?

265
00:18:26,445 --> 00:18:28,445
Could I ask you something?

266
00:18:31,485 --> 00:18:33,365
Where's my scent? Here you are,
m'lady.

267
00:18:37,725 --> 00:18:39,125
Oh!
How stupid of me.

268
00:18:39,565 --> 00:18:41,725
That was my clumsiness.
No, it was me.

269
00:18:41,765 --> 00:18:45,205
Damn! Now the room's going to smell
like a tart's boudoir.

270
00:18:46,925 --> 00:18:48,925
I'll clean it up.

271
00:18:49,645 --> 00:18:52,645
Is it all gone?
I'm afraid so.

272
00:18:52,685 --> 00:18:56,205
It means a trip to Mr Roberts.
There's no-one nearer who sells it here.

273
00:18:56,245 --> 00:18:58,445
It's only York.
I could go, if you like.

274
00:18:59,485 --> 00:19:00,645
In fact...

275
00:19:01,365 --> 00:19:02,485
What?

276
00:19:02,525 --> 00:19:07,125
Lady Rose asked me if I'd chaperone
her to a the dansant in York today.

277
00:19:07,165 --> 00:19:12,165
She didn't want to ask her ladyship
but she thought she'd get away with
it if I went with her.

278
00:19:12,205 --> 00:19:15,525
And what did you say? That I couldn't
go without your permission.

279
00:19:16,605 --> 00:19:19,525
Well, it is rather slow here for a
girl her age.

280
00:19:21,245 --> 00:19:23,645
Go but make sure you keep her out of
trouble.

281
00:19:27,885 --> 00:19:31,405
Go on. I know Gwen'd appreciate it.

282
00:19:31,925 --> 00:19:34,685
I don't see why. I didn't know her
that well.

283
00:19:34,725 --> 00:19:36,485
But you can sign it, surely?

284
00:19:41,365 --> 00:19:44,605
And why don't you come over tonight?
We haven't seen you in a while.

285
00:19:45,685 --> 00:19:48,805
You're very friendly.
Aren't I usually friendly?

286
00:19:48,845 --> 00:19:53,445
No. You're not discourteous,
Mr Bates, I'll give you that

287
00:19:53,485 --> 00:19:57,645
but you're not friendly, except to
Anna, of course.

288
00:19:59,885 --> 00:20:02,165
Well, I shall try to do better
in future.

289
00:20:03,165 --> 00:20:05,165
See you tonight.

290
00:20:08,765 --> 00:20:10,565
Mr Branson, Your ladyship.

291
00:20:10,845 --> 00:20:13,845
Sorry if I'm late. I had to call on
old Fairclough at Roundhills.

292
00:20:13,885 --> 00:20:15,565
You're not at all late.

293
00:20:15,605 --> 00:20:20,125
Now I asked Branson to come here
because I have an idea.

294
00:20:20,165 --> 00:20:21,965
Granny, you must call him Tom.

295
00:20:22,245 --> 00:20:25,405
I thought I could call him Branson
again now that he's the agent.

296
00:20:25,445 --> 00:20:26,965
Well, you can't. I don't mind.

297
00:20:27,005 --> 00:20:32,685
No. I see I'm beaten but, oh, how I
sympathise with King Canute.

298
00:20:32,725 --> 00:20:35,965
Now what is this idea?

299
00:20:36,005 --> 00:20:41,405
Well, Mary, either YOU or your baby
son own half of Downton.

300
00:20:41,445 --> 00:20:44,525
I want you to have a say in the
running of it.

301
00:20:45,445 --> 00:20:47,325
It's just what you need.

302
00:20:47,365 --> 00:20:50,485
But didn't last night's dinner
disabuse you of that scheme?

303
00:20:50,525 --> 00:20:57,485
Well, that's the point. I want er...
Tom. TOM to be your instructor.

304
00:20:57,525 --> 00:21:01,885
What? Well, take Mary, you know,
on your rounds.

305
00:21:01,925 --> 00:21:04,685
Let her learn the farmers'
difficulties.

306
00:21:05,925 --> 00:21:07,525
Mmm.

307
00:21:07,565 --> 00:21:13,005
Explain the crops and the live,
the livestock.

308
00:21:13,045 --> 00:21:16,885
You know, let her see the problems
facing the estate.

309
00:21:17,285 --> 00:21:20,045
And are we to do all this without
telling Papa?

310
00:21:20,085 --> 00:21:22,285
Isn't that rather underhand?

311
00:21:22,325 --> 00:21:26,485
There can be too much truth in any
relationship.

312
00:21:29,765 --> 00:21:31,485
Mrs Crawley.

313
00:21:36,005 --> 00:21:37,645
We weren't expecting you.

314
00:21:37,685 --> 00:21:41,485
Her ladyship is lunching with Lady
Ingram, his lordship is walking,

315
00:21:41,525 --> 00:21:44,725
Lady Mary's at the Dower House and
Lady Edith is in London.

316
00:21:44,765 --> 00:21:47,925
As a matter of fact,
it's YOU I came to see.

317
00:21:48,525 --> 00:21:54,045
To talk about Charles Grigg. You know he's
got a job at the Opera House in Belfast.

318
00:21:55,765 --> 00:21:58,565
He's so anxious to talk to you
before he goes.

319
00:21:58,605 --> 00:22:00,925
Then he is in for a disappointment.

320
00:22:00,965 --> 00:22:04,365
He says he is resolved to put his
dishonesty behind him.

321
00:22:04,405 --> 00:22:05,925
Is he now?

322
00:22:06,685 --> 00:22:08,445
I know it's more than that.

323
00:22:09,845 --> 00:22:13,525
He told me he'd caused you great
unhappiness but he said it was not
his fault.

324
00:22:14,205 --> 00:22:16,205
He was always a liar.

325
00:22:17,565 --> 00:22:19,565
I see.

326
00:22:23,005 --> 00:22:26,005
It seems a pity not to take the
chance to end a quarrel.

327
00:22:26,045 --> 00:22:28,405
Isn't it better than to let things
fester?

328
00:22:28,445 --> 00:22:30,925
I don't mean to speak out of turn,
Mrs Crawley,

329
00:22:30,965 --> 00:22:35,645
but you will, I think, accept that
any difference between Mr Grigg and
me is my concern.

330
00:22:35,685 --> 00:22:37,685
Of course it is.

331
00:22:39,365 --> 00:22:41,365
I'm sorry.

332
00:22:46,365 --> 00:22:48,365
Thank you, Carson.

333
00:22:49,485 --> 00:22:51,485
Good day.

334
00:23:00,107 --> 00:23:02,027
Shall I make us some coffee?

335
00:23:02,067 --> 00:23:03,947
You are so domesticated.

336
00:23:04,107 --> 00:23:05,827
No, not really.

337
00:23:05,867 --> 00:23:08,587
Monk just lays it all out. I only
have to pour in the water.

338
00:23:08,627 --> 00:23:11,907
I mean it. Compared to Papa you're a
famous chef.

339
00:23:12,347 --> 00:23:14,347
Really. He can't boil a kettle.

340
00:23:15,027 --> 00:23:20,667
If the servants left, he'd be found
in a passage, dead, arms stretched
out looking for the kitchen.

341
00:23:20,707 --> 00:23:22,387
Well, I hope you can boil a kettle.

342
00:23:22,427 --> 00:23:26,067
Life with me won't be quite what
you're used to.

343
00:23:27,507 --> 00:23:32,827
How are things going? Ah, well, at
least my lawyer thinks so.

344
00:23:33,467 --> 00:23:35,307
Are you sure about this?

345
00:23:35,347 --> 00:23:39,787
The Royal Family convulses the
nation by ceasing to be German and
you want to take it up.

346
00:23:41,027 --> 00:23:43,907
Does it matter that people will hate
you for it?

347
00:23:44,787 --> 00:23:47,907
Will you hate me? I will love you
more than ever.

348
00:23:47,947 --> 00:23:50,827
That's all right, then. I'm pretty
tough.

349
00:23:51,387 --> 00:23:55,147
I may not be used to the splendours of Downton
but I do know how to look after myself.

350
00:23:55,187 --> 00:23:59,107
Would you like to see Downton?
Where did that come from?

351
00:23:59,427 --> 00:24:03,747
Well, I was thinking if you are
nearly German and nearly divorced,

352
00:24:03,787 --> 00:24:06,467
perhaps the family should know you a
bit.

353
00:24:08,187 --> 00:24:11,227
It won't be settled tomorrow, my
darling, I can't pretend it will.

354
00:24:11,267 --> 00:24:13,547
Don't put me off.

355
00:24:13,587 --> 00:24:16,427
Aren't you curious about my
childhood home?

356
00:24:16,987 --> 00:24:19,427
I'm curious about everything to do
with you.

357
00:24:21,067 --> 00:24:25,027
BUT my situation would frighten them
and we don't want that.

358
00:24:26,147 --> 00:24:30,387
I know. Mama's giving a house party
next month. Why don't you come?

359
00:24:31,387 --> 00:24:33,507
Then you can blend in and look
around

360
00:24:33,547 --> 00:24:38,107
without anyone asking too many questions
and Mama won't mind, she likes you.

361
00:24:38,147 --> 00:24:40,147
But your father doesn't.

362
00:24:40,227 --> 00:24:43,787
He doesn't know you.
He'll like you when he does.

363
00:24:46,547 --> 00:24:51,507
Now, I must gulp my coffee down and
run if I'm to catch the three o'clock.

364
00:24:51,547 --> 00:24:53,547
I can't persuade you to stay?

365
00:25:01,507 --> 00:25:06,347
But, I will say this, it's getting
harder and harder to say no.

366
00:25:54,187 --> 00:25:56,187
Oh, look, we'll take that table.

367
00:25:56,587 --> 00:26:00,027
I'm not sure we should be here,
m'lady. It seems unsuitable to me.

368
00:26:00,067 --> 00:26:03,787
Don't call me m'lady, call me Rose.
I can't!

369
00:26:03,827 --> 00:26:05,827
Well, then don't call me anything.

370
00:26:10,067 --> 00:26:11,947
Seen her down there?
Yes?

371
00:26:11,987 --> 00:26:17,107
Well, it is a the dansant, let's have
a cup of tea. All right, tea.

372
00:26:19,107 --> 00:26:23,347
Oh, but perhaps with something
special in mine? Right you are, Miss.

373
00:26:23,387 --> 00:26:27,027
Listen! It's the one-step!

374
00:26:28,067 --> 00:26:31,067
Oh, why are they being so slow?

375
00:26:33,067 --> 00:26:38,747
Really, m'lady. You mustn't be so
obvious. Why not? It's working.

376
00:26:41,547 --> 00:26:44,107
I don't suppose you'd care to dance?

377
00:26:44,147 --> 00:26:47,907
Then you'd be wrong because I'd
absolutely love to.

378
00:26:48,267 --> 00:26:53,187
Shouldn't we be introduced?
The name's Sam Thawley. How do?

379
00:26:53,227 --> 00:26:54,947
I'm Rose...Smith.

380
00:26:56,387 --> 00:26:58,187
Hello. I hope you know how to do
this.

381
00:26:58,227 --> 00:27:00,547
You're in luck. They call me Twinkle
Toes.

382
00:27:07,707 --> 00:27:11,507
Hello, Anna. Jimmy, What are you
doing here?

383
00:27:11,547 --> 00:27:15,187
I was just picking up some things for
Mrs Patmore. Shall we dance?

384
00:27:17,027 --> 00:27:20,307
We'd better, if only to check Lady
Rose isn't getting in too deep.

385
00:27:26,467 --> 00:27:30,507
I'm under gardener for Lord Ellis
near Easingwold. What about you?

386
00:27:30,547 --> 00:27:33,467
I'm at... I'm at Downton Abbey.

387
00:27:33,507 --> 00:27:36,267
Oh, yeah? What do you do? Are you a
lady's maid?

388
00:27:36,307 --> 00:27:40,267
You sound posh enough.
Do I? I'm so pleased. I've...

389
00:27:40,307 --> 00:27:43,027
I've worked and worked on my accent.

390
00:27:43,067 --> 00:27:46,867
Ah, you've done well. You could pass
for a real lady never mind a lady's maid.

391
00:27:46,907 --> 00:27:53,347
I'm not a lady's maid yet. Anna is,
the girl I'm with, but I wonder if
I'm clever enough.

392
00:27:53,387 --> 00:27:58,427
Ah, well, you won't be a housemaid
forever. I expect you've got a few
young farmers hanging about.

393
00:27:58,467 --> 00:28:01,547
I couldn't say.
You won't say, you mean.

394
00:28:02,787 --> 00:28:06,587
James? How how how did you find us?

395
00:28:06,627 --> 00:28:10,507
I saw you across the square, m'lady.
Rose was saying what fun it is to be
out like this.

396
00:28:10,547 --> 00:28:13,027
I thought I'd pop in to see what the
craic was.

397
00:28:13,067 --> 00:28:15,147
It's a good craic, isn't it, Sam?

398
00:28:15,187 --> 00:28:17,187
Well, it is with you in my arms.

399
00:28:23,187 --> 00:28:25,187
I love the view from up here.

400
00:28:26,347 --> 00:28:29,187
If you know the view, all the
better.

401
00:28:29,227 --> 00:28:31,507
Follow that hedge.

402
00:28:31,547 --> 00:28:35,427
To the left of it is Oakwood Farm,
to the right is all farmed by us.

403
00:28:36,267 --> 00:28:38,667
Do we want to take over Oakwood
Farm? No.

404
00:28:38,707 --> 00:28:41,307
The Olds are good tenants and hard
workers.

405
00:28:44,627 --> 00:28:46,067
Mm.

406
00:28:46,587 --> 00:28:48,867
There is one subject we ought to
discuss.

407
00:28:49,907 --> 00:28:53,747
I know your position isn't settled
but we should. Go on.

408
00:28:54,627 --> 00:28:58,547
The death duties. If you are the
heir, it won't change them.

409
00:28:59,307 --> 00:29:03,267
There's no special treatment for
widows. You do not surprise me.

410
00:29:04,107 --> 00:29:07,347
It seems odd, really, that you have
to pay just as much tax

411
00:29:07,387 --> 00:29:10,407
as if he'd left it all to Mrs
Tiggywinkle down the road.

412
00:29:10,441 --> 00:29:12,441
Mm. But that's how it works.

413
00:29:14,641 --> 00:29:16,641
So, what are we to do?

414
00:29:18,201 --> 00:29:21,401
Your father believes we should sell
land and pay it off in one lump.

415
00:29:22,041 --> 00:29:25,681
But you don't?
I want to know what you think.

416
00:29:42,841 --> 00:29:45,641
I've some good tickets for me and
Ivy.

417
00:29:45,681 --> 00:29:48,761
To see Phyllis Dare? I don't believe
you.

418
00:29:48,801 --> 00:29:51,841
Well, I have. It cost a packet and
no mistake.

419
00:29:52,881 --> 00:29:55,201
Does Ivy know? Not yet.

420
00:29:55,241 --> 00:29:57,521
Mate, let's have a dance, eh?

421
00:29:57,561 --> 00:30:00,881
Sorry, I'm dancing with her. I said
let her dance with me.

422
00:30:00,921 --> 00:30:03,081
I don't want to dance with you.

423
00:30:03,121 --> 00:30:05,121
How do you know if you've not even
tried it?

424
00:30:05,161 --> 00:30:08,281
Don't tell me you're falling for her
at last? She will be pleased.

425
00:30:08,321 --> 00:30:10,481
I said leave her alone.
That's the idea.

426
00:30:10,521 --> 00:30:12,521
Out the way! Come on, then.

427
00:30:14,521 --> 00:30:18,961
Sam are you OK?
Get off me. Get off my hand!

428
00:30:21,241 --> 00:30:26,601
Get off him! Sam! Get off! Get off!
Sam! Get her out of here!

429
00:30:26,641 --> 00:30:29,241
Get off him! Come here, you.
James!

430
00:30:29,761 --> 00:30:31,961
I can't leave when he's fighting to
protect me.

431
00:30:32,001 --> 00:30:33,761
You can, or do you want to be
arrested?

432
00:30:33,801 --> 00:30:36,081
We need to get out of here.

433
00:30:36,121 --> 00:30:37,961
That settles it.
Come on! Go, go, go!

434
00:30:44,041 --> 00:30:48,481
Mr Molesley, is that you? Come in
and have a cup of tea.

435
00:30:48,521 --> 00:30:52,721
Mr Bates thought I might be welcome.
And so you are. Sit down.

436
00:30:52,761 --> 00:30:56,801
Mr Molesley, I'm glad you're here.

437
00:30:59,521 --> 00:31:02,481
I was going through my desk this
afternoon

438
00:31:02,521 --> 00:31:08,441
and I came upon that note of mine
and, er, if you're willing, I'd like
to pay it off now.

439
00:31:08,681 --> 00:31:11,921
What note? Well, you lent me some
money when I was first came here

440
00:31:11,961 --> 00:31:15,921
and I'm sorry I haven't paid it back before
now but the truth is I'm afraid I forgot.

441
00:31:15,961 --> 00:31:22,281
I don't remember anything about it.
£30! Aren't you the lucky one!

442
00:31:22,321 --> 00:31:23,961
Surely there must be some mistake?

443
00:31:24,001 --> 00:31:26,481
You'll not deny that's your
signature Plain as day.

444
00:31:26,521 --> 00:31:30,001
How was your trip to York?
Never mind that, what's going on?

445
00:31:30,041 --> 00:31:34,241
Mr Bates has remembered he owed £30
to Mr Molesley. What?

446
00:31:34,281 --> 00:31:38,761
But, but, but, no er...
There you are and thank you for
coming to my aid when I needed it.

447
00:31:39,801 --> 00:31:44,601
Now, I must go. I have some things
to do before his lordship comes up.

448
00:31:46,241 --> 00:31:47,681
Thank you, Mr Molesley.

449
00:31:51,601 --> 00:31:54,241
Mr Carson, can I have a word?
Certainly, James.

450
00:31:59,881 --> 00:32:01,881
Why did you do that?

451
00:32:03,601 --> 00:32:06,841
You have put up with so much that I
couldn't change

452
00:32:06,881 --> 00:32:11,841
so if there is ever the slightest thing
I can make better for you, then I will.

453
00:32:12,121 --> 00:32:18,161
But how did you manage it? Don't I
keep telling you? Prison was an education.

454
00:32:25,001 --> 00:32:28,041
Take her to the theatre?
It is my half day.

455
00:32:28,361 --> 00:32:32,921
But not hers and how do we know
she'd want to go. She'll want to go.

456
00:32:32,961 --> 00:32:35,801
She could change her half day.
Oh, I don't know.

457
00:32:35,841 --> 00:32:38,441
It's turning into Liberty Hall round
here.

458
00:32:39,241 --> 00:32:41,041
Are we entertaining that night?

459
00:32:41,081 --> 00:32:44,681
I'm not cooking. They'll all be
dining with Lady Lawson at Brough Hall.

460
00:32:44,721 --> 00:32:47,081
Very well but no lingering.

461
00:32:47,121 --> 00:32:50,681
You can go and give her the good
news. Thank you, Mr Carson.

462
00:32:56,681 --> 00:32:58,881
I hope he doesn't break her heart.

463
00:32:58,921 --> 00:33:03,001
We must all have our hearts broken
once or twice before we're done.

464
00:33:03,041 --> 00:33:05,161
True enough, Mrs Hughes.

465
00:33:08,681 --> 00:33:12,961
Strange to think the theatre was
part of my life at one time.

466
00:33:14,681 --> 00:33:16,681
Yours and Mr Griggs.

467
00:33:19,281 --> 00:33:21,681
You know he's going to work in
Belfast?

468
00:33:21,721 --> 00:33:25,961
I had heard. He's leaving the
village in the morning.

469
00:33:26,001 --> 00:33:29,081
The 11 o'clock train.
What's that to me?

470
00:33:29,761 --> 00:33:35,081
I'll tell you what it is. It's an
open wound.

471
00:33:35,121 --> 00:33:38,401
I don't know why but I do know this:

472
00:33:39,281 --> 00:33:41,761
You'd do better to stitch it up
and let it heal.

473
00:33:48,561 --> 00:33:52,161
I'm sorry, m'lord, I didn't hear the
gong. It's all right. It hasn't gone yet.

474
00:33:52,201 --> 00:33:54,281
I was hoping to catch you.

475
00:33:54,321 --> 00:33:57,841
Of course I understand how tiresome
it must have been for Anna

476
00:33:57,881 --> 00:34:01,681
when Edna came back and was suddenly
the senior lady's maid.

477
00:34:01,721 --> 00:34:03,721
M'lord? Ask her to go easy.

478
00:34:03,761 --> 00:34:07,281
I'm sorry, m'lord, but I don't
understand what you're saying.

479
00:34:07,321 --> 00:34:11,761
Her ladyship appears to think
there's been some bad feeling
between Braithwaite and Anna.

480
00:34:11,801 --> 00:34:15,281
That Anna has been a little unkind.
Not that I know of.

481
00:34:15,321 --> 00:34:19,841
Look, I don't want to make a thing
of it. I'm just asking for a little
consideration, that's all.

482
00:34:19,881 --> 00:34:23,001
And you shall have it, m'lord.
Good. That's all I needed to hear.

483
00:34:23,041 --> 00:34:25,041
Ah, there's the gong now.

484
00:34:29,441 --> 00:34:32,441
I just can't believe you're letting
me go. Do you really mean it?

485
00:34:32,481 --> 00:34:34,481
No, I was having you on.

486
00:34:35,481 --> 00:34:38,681
Oh, don't be so soft. I've said you
can go and you can go.

487
00:34:38,721 --> 00:34:40,881
What should I wear? Clothes.

488
00:34:40,921 --> 00:34:46,201
But I haven't got anything right.
Not for the theatre in York.
It's not Covent Garden.

489
00:34:46,241 --> 00:34:49,561
What? Oh, never mind. We'll make you
look presentable.

490
00:34:49,601 --> 00:34:51,761
Now get that parsley!

491
00:34:54,161 --> 00:34:57,481
I don't know why she's so excited.
It's only a bloomin' play.

492
00:35:04,361 --> 00:35:10,921
He's just as keen on her as ever.
We'll see. Nothing's as changeable
as a young man's heart.

493
00:35:11,281 --> 00:35:13,281
Take hope and a warning from that.

494
00:35:48,902 --> 00:35:50,902
I'll get it.

495
00:35:55,452 --> 00:35:58,932
Yes? I was wondering if I could have
a word with the housemaid Rose.

496
00:35:58,972 --> 00:36:01,572
The housemaid Rose? The maid Rose
who works here.

497
00:36:01,612 --> 00:36:03,892
Look, chum. I'll deal with it, Mr
Barrow.

498
00:36:04,972 --> 00:36:06,732
Hello, Mr Thawley.

499
00:36:08,012 --> 00:36:09,852
Well, I'll leave you to it.

500
00:36:11,732 --> 00:36:13,772
I've got to know she's all right.

501
00:36:13,812 --> 00:36:16,732
Rose is a bit busy just now but I'll
tell her you asked.

502
00:36:16,772 --> 00:36:18,492
I don't mind waiting.

503
00:36:18,892 --> 00:36:21,532
Can I er... Can I come in?

504
00:36:21,692 --> 00:36:25,092
If you'll just stay there, I'll...
I'll see what she's up to.

505
00:36:29,932 --> 00:36:32,572
Anna? What? What is it?

506
00:36:32,612 --> 00:36:37,212
The man you were dancing with, Sam
Thawley, he's downstairs.

507
00:36:38,332 --> 00:36:40,092
But why? What what does he want?

508
00:36:40,132 --> 00:36:42,692
He says he's come to make sure that
you're all right.

509
00:36:43,732 --> 00:36:47,772
What have you done with him? Left him in the
yard before he asks the others any questions.

510
00:36:47,812 --> 00:36:51,492
But what can I say? What can I do?

511
00:36:52,852 --> 00:36:54,852
Come with me. I've got an idea.

512
00:37:16,572 --> 00:37:19,452
This is so very kind of you.
I had to come.

513
00:37:19,492 --> 00:37:25,012
You know, I had to see that you were
...er, you know, all right after
that ruckus.

514
00:37:25,812 --> 00:37:28,652
And I...and I am, thanks to you.

515
00:37:28,692 --> 00:37:32,732
I never saw anything so brave as the
way you punched that horrid man.

516
00:37:32,772 --> 00:37:34,772
I'd do more than that for you.

517
00:37:37,132 --> 00:37:39,132
How very flattering.

518
00:37:39,532 --> 00:37:42,572
Where did you grow up? You don't
sound very Yorkshire.

519
00:37:42,612 --> 00:37:45,412
Don't I? That's...

520
00:37:45,452 --> 00:37:50,372
that's because my life's been sort of
spent all over the place.

521
00:37:50,412 --> 00:37:53,372
North, south, east, west...

522
00:37:54,532 --> 00:37:56,812
Home's the place that I love best.

523
00:37:57,852 --> 00:38:01,172
What?
That's how the song goes.

524
00:38:02,852 --> 00:38:04,932
Yes, I...I suppose it is.

525
00:38:08,092 --> 00:38:10,092
Would you let me call on you again?

526
00:38:10,972 --> 00:38:15,972
Well... Look, I know I'm not good
enough for you, I can see that

527
00:38:16,012 --> 00:38:19,652
but I'm a steady chap, ask Lord
Ellis's agent.

528
00:38:19,692 --> 00:38:23,572
He'd give me a good reference. I'm
sure but it's...it's something else.

529
00:38:24,292 --> 00:38:27,972
Do you remember wondering if any of
the local farmers were after me?

530
00:38:30,732 --> 00:38:32,892
Yeah. Well, that's the thing, I...

531
00:38:32,932 --> 00:38:37,332
There is one farmer and I didn't want
to spoil this afternoon by mentioning it

532
00:38:37,372 --> 00:38:39,452
but I have rather given him my word.

533
00:38:43,332 --> 00:38:47,772
Well, that's me back in my box,
then. I hope you understand.

534
00:38:49,372 --> 00:38:52,732
Course I do. He's a lucky bloke,
that's all I'll say.

535
00:38:55,012 --> 00:38:58,292
Well, good luck, then.
It's been nice knowing you.

536
00:39:00,532 --> 00:39:02,532
You too.

537
00:39:05,812 --> 00:39:08,172
Mr Thawley? Sam?

538
00:39:10,812 --> 00:39:17,132
When you do find someone, someone
much nicer and better than I am...

539
00:39:18,812 --> 00:39:20,812
..she'll be a very lucky girl.

540
00:39:34,412 --> 00:39:37,892
What the - Say nothing and I'll be
your friend forever.

541
00:39:44,812 --> 00:39:48,492
Is Lord Grantham in the drawing
room? Yes, m'lady. Thank you.

542
00:39:57,772 --> 00:39:59,772
Ah, here she is.

543
00:39:59,932 --> 00:40:03,172
Oh, darling. Ah, are you only just
back?

544
00:40:03,212 --> 00:40:06,732
We sat forever outside Peterborough.
We never found out why.

545
00:40:07,652 --> 00:40:10,292
Shall I change?
No, don't bother. It's only us.

546
00:40:10,332 --> 00:40:13,732
And who are we to warrant any
courtesy? Don't be difficult, Granny.

547
00:40:14,292 --> 00:40:16,212
Where's Rose? Have we lost her?

548
00:40:17,412 --> 00:40:19,852
I'm so sorry I'm late.
Never mind, never mind.

549
00:40:23,532 --> 00:40:27,772
Now I've got you all here, and before
Carson comes in, I have something to say.

550
00:40:27,812 --> 00:40:31,212
I had a letter today from Murray.

551
00:40:31,332 --> 00:40:33,972
Wait just a moment while I finish.

552
00:40:34,012 --> 00:40:37,452
Shall I leave, my lord?
No. You might as well hear this.

553
00:40:38,132 --> 00:40:41,252
Murray has taken Matthew's letter to
various authorities

554
00:40:41,292 --> 00:40:46,052
and their conclusion is that it
demonstrates testamentary intention.
What's that?

555
00:40:46,092 --> 00:40:50,012
It means that the writer intended
the document to serve as a will.

556
00:40:50,052 --> 00:40:55,412
So, the bequest stands? Yes. Mary
owns half the estate.

557
00:40:57,012 --> 00:41:02,132
That sounds like a very good result.
And now we should go into dinner
before Mrs Patmore blows a gasket.

558
00:41:07,612 --> 00:41:09,892
Dearest Papa, I hope you're not too
disappointed.

559
00:41:09,932 --> 00:41:11,772
Don't be silly. Not at all.

560
00:41:11,812 --> 00:41:15,052
Perhaps the three of us can sit down
tomorrow and talk properly.

561
00:41:15,092 --> 00:41:17,772
Of course but the main problem is a
simple one: tax.

562
00:41:17,812 --> 00:41:21,252
But that's it, Tom's told me you
want to sell land to pay for it.

563
00:41:22,092 --> 00:41:25,452
It's the only way, I'm afraid.
Yes, but, you see, I don't agree.

564
00:41:27,532 --> 00:41:31,252
Anyway, let's not talk about it now.
We ought to join the others.

565
00:41:37,492 --> 00:41:39,692
If you've put her up to this...

566
00:41:39,732 --> 00:41:45,412
I haven't put her up to anything but
you won't keep her quiet, not now
the bit's between her teeth.

567
00:41:45,452 --> 00:41:47,452
Don't think that you will.

568
00:41:49,492 --> 00:41:52,612
I don't know what he meant.
You must have said something.

569
00:41:53,652 --> 00:41:55,452
But I haven't said anything.

570
00:41:56,732 --> 00:42:00,132
I warned her off Mr Barrow, that's
all, but I wasn't having a go at her.

571
00:42:00,172 --> 00:42:02,852
All I can tell you is that she has
managed to take offence.

572
00:42:02,892 --> 00:42:04,892
She can't have.

573
00:42:12,012 --> 00:42:14,092
Mr Barrow, what are you doing down
here?

574
00:42:14,132 --> 00:42:17,052
Will you come up this moment and
help me serve the wine?

575
00:42:17,092 --> 00:42:18,692
Sorry, Mr Carson, I'm on my way.

576
00:42:34,332 --> 00:42:36,332
What's so funny?

577
00:42:37,812 --> 00:42:39,812
Nothing, Mr Bates.

578
00:42:40,572 --> 00:42:42,572
Nothing at all.

579
00:43:41,172 --> 00:43:43,172
Good morning, Carson.

580
00:43:44,492 --> 00:43:49,412
I hope I'm not in the way.
Not a bit. You remember Mr Grigg?

581
00:43:53,612 --> 00:43:57,132
Hello, Charlie. Good of you to come.

582
00:44:04,852 --> 00:44:07,892
I'm delighted but I'm not surprised.

583
00:44:08,732 --> 00:44:12,012
Aren't you? Because I'm astonished.

584
00:44:13,452 --> 00:44:17,212
But why did you never speak of Alice
when we last met before the war?

585
00:44:17,252 --> 00:44:21,852
What was the point? She chose you
all those years ago and that was it.

586
00:44:21,892 --> 00:44:25,092
Why bring it up?
Because it wasn't IT.

587
00:44:25,612 --> 00:44:27,852
She chose me but it never worked.

588
00:44:27,892 --> 00:44:32,172
She's dead now, anyway,
but it was never IT.

589
00:44:34,932 --> 00:44:38,212
I didn't know she was dead.
Five year ago.

590
00:44:38,252 --> 00:44:42,252
We'd separated long before but I
went to see her in St Thomas's.

591
00:44:42,972 --> 00:44:45,572
Do you know what she said?

592
00:44:45,612 --> 00:44:50,532
She said, 'Charlie Carson was the
better man. I could have loved him.

593
00:44:50,572 --> 00:44:55,332
I did love him, really, but I was a
fool and couldn't see it.'

594
00:44:56,452 --> 00:44:59,732
Did she say that? Honestly?

595
00:44:59,772 --> 00:45:01,692
That she loved you? Aye, she did

596
00:45:04,332 --> 00:45:07,012
and she wanted me to tell you,
if I saw you again.

597
00:45:07,212 --> 00:45:10,332
There we are. We could have made a
go of it, you know.

598
00:45:11,132 --> 00:45:15,372
As long as you know that it were her
choice. I never set out to take her off you.

599
00:45:15,412 --> 00:45:17,572
All aboard!

600
00:45:17,612 --> 00:45:20,892
I am sorry to interrupt but I think
you must get aboard.

601
00:45:24,652 --> 00:45:28,092
I can't tell you how grateful I am,
Mrs Crawley. Very good luck.

602
00:45:28,132 --> 00:45:30,132
Thank you.

603
00:45:32,772 --> 00:45:36,972
I doubt we'll meet again
but can we shake on it?

604
00:45:38,372 --> 00:45:42,132
We've known some ups and downs
together, it's true,

605
00:45:42,172 --> 00:45:44,132
but if this is goodbye...

606
00:45:44,172 --> 00:45:46,092
let's part as friends, eh?

607
00:45:46,132 --> 00:45:48,132
All right.

608
00:45:49,892 --> 00:45:53,372
I wish you well. Likewise...

609
00:45:53,412 --> 00:45:55,412
..Charlie.

610
00:46:05,532 --> 00:46:07,572
Mrs Crawley, I should be grateful

611
00:46:07,612 --> 00:46:13,172
if you would let me know any expense
you have been put to on Mr Griggs'
behalf during his stay with you.

612
00:46:13,212 --> 00:46:17,492
Oh, no. That's completely
unnecessary. I should be grateful.

613
00:46:19,852 --> 00:46:21,852
Very well, Carson, I shall do that.

614
00:46:24,532 --> 00:46:26,532
Good day to you.

615
00:46:37,972 --> 00:46:40,052
Mr Carson?

616
00:46:40,092 --> 00:46:42,132
Shall we walk back together?

617
00:46:49,652 --> 00:46:52,532
I gather the Abbey is once more to
be a scene of great splendour.

618
00:46:52,572 --> 00:46:55,492
Who's the glamorous pirate?
Johnnie Gillingham's son.

619
00:46:55,532 --> 00:46:58,292
Stick it under my arm, if you can.
You're an angel from above.

620
00:46:58,332 --> 00:47:01,492
I don't know about that.
Is there anyone who wants to dance?

621
00:47:01,532 --> 00:47:06,612
What about it? How can grown
men throw away their fortune like
that? What could be more stupid?

622
00:47:06,652 --> 00:47:10,412
There's something about him that gets
my goat. He was just trying to be nice.

623
00:47:10,452 --> 00:47:15,572
I don't want them to spend
It seems disloyal to Matthew.

624
00:47:15,612 --> 00:47:19,323
Screaming in the servants' halls.
What a topsy-turvy world we've come to.

625
00:47:19,357 --> 00:47:22,155
